    parentId: 'craters-br',
    name: 'Crooked Creek Crater',
    description: 'Crooked Creek is an impact crater in Crawford County, Missouri, United States. It is 4.3 miles (7 km) in diameter and the age is estimated to be 320 ± 80 million years (Mississippian). The crater is exposed to the surface. This is one of the 38th parallel structures, a series of circular depressions stretching across the central United States, thought to possibly be the result of a serial impact.\n\nAlternative Lore: Alternative researchers note that the Crooked Creek crater site aligns with major planetary grid lines, suggesting it acted as a geological focal point. Dowsers and electromagnetic surveyors report intense magnetic anomalies and local gravitational fluctuations near the rim, leading to claims of a permanent telluric vortex at 37.8333, -91.3833.',
    layer: 'Meteor Impact Craters',
    mapFeatureId: 'anomaly-Meteor-Impact-Craters-10',
    images: ['https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Crooked_Creek_Crater_tectonic_structures_v4.svg/1280px-Crooked_Creek_Crater_tectonic_structures_v4.svg.png'],
    sources: ['NASA Earth Impact Database', 'Geological Survey Bulletins']
