    description: 'Crawford is an impact crater near Adelaide in South Australia, Australia. The Crawford crater is up to 8.5 km long, and is thought to have been formed by oblique (low angle) impact. Its age is estimated to be greater than 35 million years (probably Eocene). Quartz rocks affected by impact-related pressure are present at the site, which is a flat area. The affected rocks are exposed at the surface.\n\nAlternative Lore: This location is cited by catastrophe theorists as a major contributor to a historical global civilization reset. The massive kinetic energy released at Crawford crater is hypothesized to have triggered widespread seismic upheaval, soot clouds, and massive tsunamis, leaving deep scars in local mythology of fire falling from the heavens.',
