    name: 'Manicouagan Reservoir',
    description: 'Manicouagan Reservoir (also Lake Manicouagan ; French: [manikwaɡɑ̃]) is an annular lake in central Quebec, Canada, covering an area of 1,942 km2 (750 sq mi). The lake island in its centre is known as René-Levasseur Island, and its highest point is Mount Babel. The structure was created 214 (±1) million years ago, in the Late Triassic, by the impact of a meteorite 5 km (3 mi) in diameter. The lake and island are clearly seen from space and are sometimes called the "eye of Quebec" The lake has a volume of 137.9 km3 (33.1 cu mi).\n\nAlternative Lore: Ufologists and fringe researchers speculate that Manicouagan Reservoir may hide a deep, covert subterranean facility or D.U.M.B. nested within the impact-compacted bedrock. The natural geological shielding is thought to provide perfect cover for advanced aerospace tests and electromagnetic defense arrays shielded from external satellite scan telemetry.',
