    description: 'Lake Elgygytgyn, also transcripted El\'gygytgyn, (Russian and Chukchi: Эльгыгытгын) is a crater lake in Anadyrsky District, Chukotka Autonomous Okrug in northeast Siberia, about 150 km (93 mi) southeast of Chaunskaya Bay. The word "Elgygytgyn" means "white lake" in the Chukchi language. The lake is of particular interest to scientists because it has never been covered by glaciers. This has allowed the uninterrupted build-up of 400 m (1,300 ft) of sediment at the bottom of the lake, recording information on prehistoric climate change.\n\nAlternative Lore: Alternative researchers note that the Lake Elgygytgyn site aligns with major planetary grid lines, suggesting it acted as a geological focal point. Dowsers and electromagnetic surveyors report intense magnetic anomalies and local gravitational fluctuations near the rim, leading to claims of a permanent telluric vortex at 67.5000, 172.0000.',
