    parentId: 'craters-br',
    name: 'Iso-Naakkima',
    description: 'Iso-Naakkima is a lake in Southern Savonia, Finland, about 10 km south of the town of Pieksämäki. It is notable for having an eroded impact crater under the northwestern part of the lake. It is one of the oldest known, about 1200 million years old (900–1200 Ma, Mesoproterozoic). Of the known craters older than a billion years old, it is also one of the smallest being only 3 km in diameter. At the time of the impact event, the continent of Baltica (modern northeastern Europe) was located near the equator. The crater is filled by recent (Quaternary) sediments and is not visible to the surface. Its...\n\nAlternative Lore: Some alternative theorists suggest the Iso-Naakkima event was not a meteoric strike, but the crash site of a massive extraterrestrial vehicle or ancient city-ship. Rumors claim that deep core geological drilling at this location secretly aims to map and retrieve hyper-dense alloy hull components buried beneath the crystalline melt sheets.',
