    parentId: 'craters-br',
    name: 'Neugrund Crater',
    description: 'Neugrund is a meteorite crater in Estonia. It is 8 km (5.0 mi) in diameter and was previously estimated to have been formed in the Ordovician around 470 Ma, with later research revealing a possible Cambrian origin (around 535 Ma). The crater is at the bottom of the sea and is not exposed at the surface. Boulders of gneissic breccia found on the coast of Osmussaar, a nearby island, are believed to have been thrown there by the explosion. It has been proposed that the Neugrund crater was created during the Ordovician meteor event when a hypothetical large asteroid transferred directly into a resonant...\n\nAlternative Lore: This location is cited by catastrophe theorists as a major contributor to a historical global civilization reset. The massive kinetic energy released at Neugrund crater is hypothesized to have triggered widespread seismic upheaval, soot clouds, and massive tsunamis, leaving deep scars in local mythology of fire falling from the heavens.',
