    description: 'Ouarkziz (Arabic: أوركزيز) is a meteorite impact crater in Algeria. It is 3.5 kilometers in diameter and the age is estimated to be less than 70 million years (Cretaceous or younger). The crater is exposed at the surface. The Ouarkziz Impact Crater is located in northwestern Algeria, close to the border with Morocco. The crater was formed by a meteor impact less than 70 million years ago, during the late Cretaceous Period of the Mesozoic Era. Originally called Tindouf, the 3.5-kilometer wide crater has been heavily eroded since its formation; however, its circular morphology is highlighted by exposures...\n\nAlternative Lore: Ufologists and fringe researchers speculate that Ouarkziz crater may hide a deep, covert subterranean facility or D.U.M.B. nested within the impact-compacted bedrock. The natural geological shielding is thought to provide perfect cover for advanced aerospace tests and electromagnetic defense arrays shielded from external satellite scan telemetry.',
