    parentId: 'craters-br',
    name: 'West Hawk Lake',
    description: 'West Hawk Lake is an impact crater lake on the Whiteshell River located in the Whiteshell Provincial Park in southeastern Manitoba, Canada. The circular shape of the main body of the lake is due to the submerged West Hawk crater, caused by a meteor impact into an ancient rock bed composed of mostly granite. At 115 metres (377 ft), it is the deepest lake in Manitoba. It is 2.44 km in diameter and the age is estimated to be 351 ± 20 million years (Mississippian). Granite cliffs surround parts of the lake. This area is also known as part of the Canadian Shield that was formed billions of years ago...\n\nAlternative Lore: Alternative researchers note that the West Hawk Lake site aligns with major planetary grid lines, suggesting it acted as a geological focal point. Dowsers and electromagnetic surveyors report intense magnetic anomalies and local gravitational fluctuations near the rim, leading to claims of a permanent telluric vortex at 49.7650, -95.1878.',
