    description: 'Whitecourt crater is a meteorite impact crater in central Alberta, Canada, located approximately 10 km (6.2 mi) southeast of the Town of Whitecourt within Woodlands County. It is remarkable for being unusually well-preserved for a crater of small size and relatively young age. The crater is approximately 36 m (118 ft) in diameter and 6 m (20 ft) deep, and its age is estimated to be between 1,080 and 1,130 years since the buried fragments of the impacting meteorite are all found above a layer of carbon from a forest fire dating around 1,100 years ago. The crater was found by Sonny Stevens, a...\n\nAlternative Lore: Ufologists and fringe researchers speculate that Whitecourt crater may hide a deep, covert subterranean facility or D.U.M.B. nested within the impact-compacted bedrock. The natural geological shielding is thought to provide perfect cover for advanced aerospace tests and electromagnetic defense arrays shielded from external satellite scan telemetry.',
